{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 400,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for sponsoring PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To progress to the next level in understanding \nreality, we need to combine quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Einstein’s general relativity. And \nto do that, most physicists believe we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need a theory of quantum gravity .. which \nmeans we need gravitons. But it also seems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18000,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the laws of physics make it impossible to \never detect this quantum particle of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Almost like the universe is set up to keep \nthe final answer forever out of our reach."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29760,
    "dDurationMs": 13600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, can we outsmart the universe, catch \na graviton, and finally solve physics?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve got a couple quick announcements before \nwe start. First, our eternal battle against the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the algorithm continues. The best way to \nencourage YouTube to share our videos is to like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and comment - doing both really makes \na difference. And if you’re new here,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe, hit the bell, and introduce \nyourself in the comments. We’re friendly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re also offering 20% off the entire \nmerch store until January 19th, 2026. So"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if there’s anything you had your eye on there’s \na link in the description. Now onto the episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2012, the legendary physicist Freeman \nDyson gave a talk in which he speculated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82480,
    "dDurationMs": 10800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the possibility of ever detecting gravitons. He \nwas not optimistic. In his Poincare prize lecture,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he laid out how the universe seems to conspire \nto make the detection of the quantum particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99040,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity impossible. In some cases it seems to \nbe impossible for all practical experiments—the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106160,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments are just too outlandish \nto ever happen. But in other cases,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "graviton detection seems to be in-principle \nand fundamentally impossible—we’re thwarted by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116960,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the appearance of black holes or by the quantum \nvacuum from ever glimpsing a graviton. So does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe really prohibit us from ever seeing \nthe building block of the fabric of spacetime?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128720,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a reminder, the graviton is \nthe quantum particle of gravity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as the photon is the quanta of \nelectromagnetism, gluons of the strong force,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc. But the graviton is more than that—it’s the \nbuilding block of the fabric of the universe. In"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same way that an electromagnetic field \nis made of a sea of “virtual” photons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime is made of gravitons. At least, that’s \ntrue if spacetime has a quantum nature, as most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists seem to believe. All theories of \nquantum gravity require them, from string theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161360,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to loop quantum gravity. So, probably worth trying \nto spot one if we want to verify these theories."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168080,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now in the past we’ve talked about indirect \nways to test the quantum nature of gravity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but what about detecting the graviton itself? \nWell, there are two broad ways we can think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about graviton detection. On the one hand, \nwe can take what we know about detecting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classical gravitational fields and try to apply \nit to a quantized field of gravitons—basically,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190560,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to detect the gravitational effect of \na single graviton. On the other hand,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can treat gravitons like every other particle \nand use the same techniques we once used to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "squeeze out photons from the electromagnetic \nfield. By tackling both perspectives we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207200,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get a reasonably comprehensive sense of how \nimpossible graviton detection really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214400,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by trying to detect the \ngravitational effect of a single graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218640,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll think about this in terms of \nthe most sensitive gravity detectors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever built: the Laser Interferometer \nGravitational Wave Observatory—LIGO."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This pair of detectors tracks the subtle \ndifferences in phase of a pair of laser"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beams traveling along 4km perpendicular \narms. When those phases fail to line up,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it indicates a relative change in the lengths \nof those arms—a possible signature of a passing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244240,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational wave. We’ve used these \nobservatories to spot gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves from hundreds of merging black holes and \nneutron stars since the first detection in 2015."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254640,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what would it take for a laser interferometer \nalong these lines to detect a single graviton?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261040,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll follow Dyson’s argument here. LIGO is able \nto detect gravitational waves with a “strain” of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267680,
    "dDurationMs": 9680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10^-22—that’s the relative change in length it’s \nsensitive to. It corresponds to one-one thousandth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277360,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the width of a proton for the 300 back and forth \nreflections along LIGO’s 4km arms. If gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is indeed quantum then gravitational waves are \nmade of a coherent superposition of gravitons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the same way that our lasers are made \nof photons. A wave at LIGO’s strain limit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would contain at least 10^36 gravitons. \nWe’re trying to see a single graviton,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so in principle we’d need a detector \nthat’s 10^36 times more sensitive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The critical question we need to ask is \nhow precisely do we need to measure the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "variation in the length of detector arms to \npick up a single graviton event? It turns"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out that the answer is independent \nof the type of gravitational wave:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for optimal sensitivity, we need to be able to \nmeasure a length difference of order a single"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck length. This should already raise \nconcerns because the Planck length is the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance where our current understanding \nof space breaks down. But let’s proceed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The limits of measurability of literally \neverything is defined by the Heisenberg"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty principle. The principal sets \nan absolute limit on how precisely we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know complementary pairs of properties \nsimultaneously, for example the position"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or momentum of something. Let’s start \nsimple by imagining one LIGO arm as a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair of free-floating mirrors. A gravitational \nwave causes the distance between them to change,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we want to measure that changing distance. \nThat’s effectively measured by bouncing a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon between them. However that photon also \nimparts momentum on the mirrors, inducing an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty in the mirror position. The more \nprecisely we want to measure those positions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the higher the frequency of light we need, \ntherefore the more momentum we have to impart. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391520,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position-momentum uncertainty relation can be used \nto relate the limit of the position measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the imparted mirror momentum. We can then \nconvert this relation to one involving time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in order to accurately measure the separation \nof the mirrors, the measurement has to happen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fast—faster than the mirrors move out \nof position due to the act of measuring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short, we can maximize the precision \nof our measurement of the mirror separation in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two ways: by reducing the distance between the \nmirrors—that reduces measurement time. And by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing the mass of the mirrors, heavy mirrors \nmove more slowly when bumped by the measuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon. If we want a 1-Planck-length position \nprecision due to the passage of a graviton,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mirrors need to be massive enough and close \nenough together that they form a black hole. Well,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447520,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that sucks. By definition, the formation of an \nevent horizon prevents our distance measurement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dyson shows that we get the same issue \nvia a different argument even if we fix"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460240,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mirrors in place. In general, any \ndistance measurement on the scale of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Planck length gives us black holes, which \nmeans that direct measurement of the effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a single graviton by a LIGO-like \ndevice is fundamentally impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, let’s try something else. The \ngravitational wave approach tries to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measure the actual gravitational effect of a \nsingle graviton. But maybe treating gravitons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitationally is using a classical \nhammer to crack a quantum nut. Instead,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe we should look for gravitons by the same \nmethod as we look for other quantum particles. We"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can summarize that method in very general terms: \ncrash particles together and see what happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The physics of colliding particles is inherently \ndifferent to the interactions of classical waves,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so maybe that’s the right approach for spotting \na particle that arises from a quantum field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most obvious example of the particle \ncollision method is, surprise surprise,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particle collider. We discovered the Higgs \nboson by building a collider so large that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527520,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its collision energies could generate the \nmassive Higgs particle. And that’s the 27"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "km diameter large hadron collider. So how large \na collider do we need to generate the graviton?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542880,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gravitons are massless, so energy isn’t needed to \ngive them mass like with the Higgs. Rather, energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is needed to increase the chance of generating \none. Gravity is the weakest force by a long,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long way. It’s 24 orders of magnitude weaker \nthan the weakest of the other fundamental forces,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562800,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a fact which is itself a conundrum \ncalled the hierarchy problem,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565920,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which obviously we’ve talked about before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567760,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In quantum terms, that weakness can be expressed \nas a very small coupling constant between the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "graviton and other particles. The probability \nof generating a graviton in a particle collision"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depends on that coupling constant. Despite the \nword “constant”, the coupling factor actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increases with the energy of the interaction \nAt around a billion Joules, the coupling for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity reaches the ballpark strength of the \nother forces. So that’s the energy we need to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598560,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reach in our collisions to have a fair chance \nof producing gravitons. How big an accelerator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605280,
    "dDurationMs": 10240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do we need to reach that energy? Well the LHC \ncollisions reach a whopping millionth of a Joule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615520,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a fixed magnetic field strength, collision \nenergy scales directly with collider size. To"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to the energy to detect a graviton, \na collider with the same magnets as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628400,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "27km LHC would need to be around 3 light years in \ndiameter—much bigger than our solar system. That"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637520,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sounds insanely difficult, but it’s in-principle \npossible. And once we’ve build a graviton-factory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we get to the real challenge— it's not \ncreating gravitons but detecting them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652160,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now massless gravitons are stable, \nso we can’t rely on looking for their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655840,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay products. We’d need to see \none via its gravitational effect,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we just saw may be impossible, or when \nit’s absorbed by or scatters another particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The OG example of discovering a particle this \nway is the photoelectric effect, in which an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron is ejected from a conducting plate by \na single photon. Einstein used this effect to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "demonstrate the existence of photons as quanta \nof the electromagnetic field. So can we use a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684080,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "similar test to detect gravitons? An equivalent \ngravito-electric effect? Well in principle,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yes. A sufficiently high-frequency graviton \nwill carry enough energy to kick an electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696560,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out of an atom or out of a conducting plate. \nWe could also imagine a graviton equivalent of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Compton effect, in which electrons are \nejected from atomic orbitals. Either way,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we shift the problem from detecting gravitons \nto detecting electrons, which is pretty easy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The probability of an electron absorbing a \ngraviton to produce one of these phenomena depends"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721360,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the coupling strength of gravity. So just \nas with graviton creation, it’s super unlikely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to think about this interaction \nstrength is in terms of the cross-section."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736160,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s called a cross-section because it tells \nus how big an electron looks to a graviton—how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close the graviton needs to get to the electron \ncenter in order to hit it and get absorbed. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the graviton-electron interaction the \ncross section is proportional to the square"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Planck length. So even if we can create \na graviton in our interstellar-sized collider,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s call it the SLC—“stupidly large \ncollider”, we can probably never detect it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other way we can increase the \nlikelihood of a gravito-electric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interaction is by increasing the number of \ngravitons in the right frequency range. So"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe we amp up the power of our SLC, or \nwe find a natural source of gravitons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The theoretical physicist Stephen Weinberg \ncalculated that there is potentially a huge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791840,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantity of high-frequency gravitons being emitted \nby the Sun.These are produced by electron-graviton"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interactions in the hot, dense core of our star. \nAt the energy we need, the sun should produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804400,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about 10^24 per second. This amounts to about \n4 per meter squared per second passing through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813520,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth’s surface. You probably don’t notice \nthe several that passed through you during this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819040,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sentence alone. The interaction cross-section for \nthis is so small that these solar gravitons only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825840,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact with a particle of matter roughly once \nevery billion years or so across the entire volume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832800,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Earth. Now we could build a star-sized \ndetector and maybe get a detection every 1000"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years rather than every billion, but that \nstill feels like an unsatisfying yield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We probably actually need a stronger source \nof gravitons than a mere entire star. The best"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854720,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "option might be the collapsed core of a dead \nstar. Robert Gould estimates that a hot white"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dwarf or a neutron star would emit, respectively, \n100 and 100,000 times more gravitons than the Sun,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "though over a shorter timescale. So placing \na planet or star-sized detector near such"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873360,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a stellar remnant might net us a single \ngraviton on human timescales. Of course,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the path to building such an experiment is far \nbeyond human timescales if it’s possible at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if we were somehow able to pull this \noff, we’d face an even more basic issue:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos. These ghostly particles are notoriously \nhard to spot. Most of the Sun’s neutrinos pass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straight through the earth, and we’ve built \ndetectors out of the Antarctic ice cap to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catch the occasional rare interaction. But by \ncomparison to the shy graviton, the neutrino is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913600,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a party animal. For any of our graviton sources, \nregardless of whether it’s our Sun, a white dwarf,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920240,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or a neutron star, our detector will interact \nwith 10^34 neutrinos per single graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And distinguishing that graviton from \nthe neutrino noise seems practically impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try one more method that on the \nsurface looks promising. In the 1960s Mikhail"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gertsenshtein showed that electromagnetic and \ngravitational waves, in a strong magnetic field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can couple together. This is more promising than \nlooking for interactions between gravitons and,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952080,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say, electrons. EM and gravitational waves, \nwhich both travel at the speed of light,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can experience wave resonance that allows energy \nto transfer between them at much lower coupling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strength than is needed for the absorption of a \ngraviton by matter. This means a photon may be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970720,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transformed into a graviton and vice versa. So, if \nwe shoot a graviton through a magnetic field, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will oscillate between graviton states and photon \nstates. This is called the Gertsenshtein effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984320,
    "dDurationMs": 4876,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now to do this, we need a long hollow tube \nthreaded by a strong magnetic field passing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989196,
    "dDurationMs": 5444,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and pointed towards a source of gravitons. The \nhope is that some of those gravitons will turn"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into photons that we can detect. And, as you \nmay have guessed, the universe again conspires"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against us. The magnetic field needs to be quite \nstrong for the Gertsenschtein effect to work. How"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong? Strong enough that we have spontaneous \ncreation of matter-antimatter pairs inside the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013360,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tube. This vacuum polarization resulting from this\nlimits the coherence of EM waves in the tube,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preventing resonance with gravitational waves \nand shutting down the Gertsenschtein effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re at strike three for detecting \ngravitons. It seems the universe really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032000,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t want us to see its deepest \nstructure. But is it really impossible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or just nearly impossible? In a couple of \nthe cases we looked out the answer is …"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible. The barrier to graviton detection \nseems fundamental. The formation of black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047440,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes or the breakdown of the vacuum tells \nus that the laws of physics forbid graviton"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053360,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "detection by these methods. But in other \ncases it’s just astonishingly difficult—we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need star-sized graviton sources and detectors \nand some yet-unknown way to filter neutrinos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But next to impossible is still possible. \nI’m saying there’s a chance. And maybe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the problem is actually just that we haven’t \ncome up with a smart enough experiment yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe we have. Since Dyson’s 2012 \nlecture, we’ve detected gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083200,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves directly and we’ve made major \nadvancements in quantum technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has inspired a new generation of physicists \nto come up with a new generation of proposals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1094000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, we may be able to combine \nthe two broad approaches I mentioned:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using a LIGO-like interferometer combined with \nabsorption of gravitons, this time by a detector"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with novel quantum properties. How does that \nwork? Well that’s for an episode coming soon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111200,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we’ll find out how we really may be able to \ndetect the particle at the heart of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS. \nBrilliant is where you learn by doing,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with thousands of interactive lessons in physics, \nmath, programming, and AI. Brilliant helps build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your critical thinking skills through problem \nsolving—not memorizing. Each lesson starts by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helping you master the foundations, then levels \nyou up to increasingly challenging problems. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you’re into computation, Brilliant offers \na course on Algorithmic Thinking designed to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1147680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "help you understand the computational science \nof algorithms. In the course, you’ll tackle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real programming challenges that teach you how \nto think through algorithmic design, strategy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159680,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and optimization. To try everything Brilliant \nhas to offer go to brilliant.org/spacetime or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1167520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "click the link in the description. Brilliant’s \nalso given our viewers 20% off an annual Premium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscription, which gives you unlimited \ndaily access to everything on Brilliant."
    } ]
  } ]
}
